{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-15702",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
